WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:10.050
When we look back over the last century of
innovation in flight it’s sometimes hard

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:11.800
to believe how far we have come.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:16.980
The Wright’s first flight in 1903 was at
best a proof of concept; only managing to

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:20.550
fly 37 metres before falling ungracefully
from the sky.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.470
We often look back at this historic event
and see it as the spark that ignited a century

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:29.529
of human flight, the truth is, the event barely
registered in national media and most questioned

00:00:29.529 --> 00:00:31.140
the legitimacy of the news.

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.120
It took another 3 years of incremental improvements
and public test flights before the international

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:40.600
community began to accept their achievements
and by that stage others had begun to catch

00:00:40.600 --> 00:00:42.680
up and even surpass their designs.

00:00:42.680 --> 00:00:47.900
By 1910, Louis Blériot had flown across the
English Channel, Georges Chavez soared over

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:53.000
2 kms to clear the Alps and Glenn Curtis began
to testing planes as a platform for weapons

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:56.769
and his biplane became the first to take off
from the deck of a ship.

00:00:56.769 --> 00:01:00.609
This marked a trend for the next 35 years
of aviation history, which was dominated by

00:01:00.609 --> 00:01:05.500
war and by the time World War 2 came to a
close giant companies had been formed who

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:09.350
were mass producing planes capable of transporting
humans across the world.

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:13.340
These companies were not going to simply vanish
as the war ended and instead set their sights

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.410
on building a new commercial civilian transport
industry.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:21.150
In the final year of World War 2 over 4 thousand
Douglas DC-3s had been built and many of these

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:23.770
would go on to be converted for civilian use.

00:01:23.770 --> 00:01:28.540
The DC-3 is still the most produced airliner
in history with over 16,000 built and some

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:32.400
are even still in service across the world,
but it’s slowly being caught up by the Boeing

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:37.560
737, which has sold so many units that at
any single point there is an average of 2000

00:01:37.560 --> 00:01:39.550
737s in the air.

00:01:39.550 --> 00:01:44.390
The 737 made it’s debut in 1968 and it’s
design has essentially become the template

00:01:44.390 --> 00:01:47.330
for which most jet airliners have been built
on since.

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:52.400
The initial design of the 737 had the engines
mounted on the tail, similar to the DC-9,

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.630
which the 737 was competing with, but placing
the engines here reduced the amount of space

00:01:56.630 --> 00:02:00.380
available towards the rear of the cabin and
mounting the engine pods tight against the

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:04.380
underside of the wing freed up space at the
back of the cabin for more passengers, which

00:02:04.380 --> 00:02:07.710
was important for this narrow and short body,
short haul plane.

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:11.849
It also reduced the bending load on the wings,
counter-acting the upward bending load caused

00:02:11.849 --> 00:02:12.849
by lift.

00:02:12.849 --> 00:02:17.640
The success of this design has allowed the
737 to stay in service for over half a century

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.950
with incremental improvements and today it’s
so popular that most budget airlines like

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:24.719
Ryanair and Southwest airline use no other
plane.

00:02:24.719 --> 00:02:27.170
It’s engines have got gradually larger and
more powerful.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:31.909
It’s cabin got larger as traffic increased,
wingets were introduced to the wing to reduce

00:02:31.909 --> 00:02:36.810
induced drag and later this year the latest
iteration of the 737, which has already sold

00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:42.370
over 3400 units, will make it’s debut with
new split winglets, more efficient engines,

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:47.439
an improved flight deck and the modern cabin
interior developed for the 787 dreamliner.

00:02:47.439 --> 00:02:51.760
This theme of incremental improvements in
the airline industry happens for a reason.

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.980
Introducing a totally new plane design is
an incredibly risky business.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:58.769
We need to look no further than the failed
Concorde for proof of that, but even introducing

00:02:58.769 --> 00:03:03.069
a new plane series like Boeing’s 787 can
cause massive losses in revenue.

00:03:03.069 --> 00:03:07.530
The plane was plagued with delays, originally
slated to arrive in 2008, but actually made

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:12.200
its first commercial flight in 2011 and only
recently has hit it’s stride in manufacturing

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:13.200
and sales.

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:17.549
New designs are simply a risky business decision
and in general companies will play it safe

00:03:17.549 --> 00:03:18.890
and not break the mold.

00:03:18.890 --> 00:03:22.639
On top of this a plane’s service life is
a huge part of its selling point.

00:03:22.639 --> 00:03:25.680
Airlines want to buy planes that maintain
their value over the years and can last them

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.010
a significant amount of time with minimal
maintenance, so manufacturers have made effort

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:34.040
to increase the service life of these planes,
which in turn has increased the cycle times

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.079
between new iterations of planes.

00:03:36.079 --> 00:03:38.300
Making progress even slower again.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.840
With the current status quo of the airline
industry.

00:03:40.840 --> 00:03:45.120
We aren’t likely to see much change any
time soon, BUT what if a new industry disrupter

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:46.120
emerged.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.389
One that could shake up the duopoly of Boeing
and Airbus to force competition and new designs?

00:03:50.389 --> 00:03:53.610
We have seen this happen in other industries
recently.

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:57.629
The energy sector is being revolutionised
by cheap solar panels, Tesla was the first

00:03:57.629 --> 00:04:01.760
successful car start up in America in over
a century and composite materials are set

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.499
to continue replacing metals in many every-day
applications.

00:04:05.499 --> 00:04:09.359
These disruptive technologies combined with
rising air traffic could raise the pressure

00:04:09.359 --> 00:04:10.409
to innovate.

00:04:10.409 --> 00:04:14.069
In this new series of videos I am going to
break down a number of future aircraft and

00:04:14.069 --> 00:04:17.420
the design challenges they need to overcome
to become a reality.

00:04:17.420 --> 00:04:22.750
Let’s first take a look at the D8, nicknamed
the Double Bubble, developed by Aurura, MIT

00:04:22.750 --> 00:04:24.140
and with the help of NASA.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:28.010
The current template of plane design at the
moment consists of a tubular fuselage.

00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:32.040
This shape is primarily there to resist the
internal pressurisation, allowing the fuselage

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.260
to expand without creating dangerous stress
concentrations.

00:04:35.260 --> 00:04:39.280
As long as we pressurise the inside of our
planes this design aspect won’t change,

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:43.780
but we can create fuselages with multiple
interconnecting tubular sections.

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:47.540
This is exactly what the D8 does, with it’s
double bubble fuselage.

00:04:47.540 --> 00:04:51.030
So let’s look at how they came up with this
design and the theory behind their design

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:52.030
choices.

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:56.410
To design this concept they actually started
off with a 737 and performed a morphing study

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.760
by gradually introducing their design goals
to the current design.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:04.620
They started by first optimising the airframe
of the current 737-800 airframe with current

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:06.090
generation improvements.

00:05:06.090 --> 00:05:08.750
They then changed the fuselage to feature
the double bubble.

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:10.920
This shortened and widened the fuselage considerably.

00:05:10.920 --> 00:05:15.750
The wider body and shaped nose allows the
body of the aircraft to generate more lift,

00:05:15.750 --> 00:05:17.400
particularly at the nose.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:21.030
This allowed the wings to get thinner and
thus reduce the drag they generate, but it

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:23.830
also meant that the tail wing could decrease
in size too.

00:05:23.830 --> 00:05:28.410
The primary purpose of the tail wing is to
generate downforce at the rear of the plane,

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:32.880
which keeps the nose of the plane up, an important
stability characteristic, but when the nose

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.360
generates it’s own lift, the importance
of the tail wing is diminished and it can

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:39.730
decrease in size, which again reduces the
drag.

00:05:39.730 --> 00:05:44.450
They then reduced the cruise speed of the
plane from 0.80 mach to 0.76 mach, which may

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:48.860
seem like a step backwards, but remember the
primary goal of this future design are to

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.640
improve efficiency.

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:54.210
This allowed the wing sweep of the plane to
decrease, if you don’t understand this go

00:05:54.210 --> 00:05:57.360
ahead and watch my “why are plane wings
angled backwards video”.

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:02.410
In the next iteration they reduced the cruise
speed again to 0.72, essentially removing

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:04.280
the wing sweep altogether.

00:06:04.280 --> 00:06:08.040
Reducing the speed of the plane reduces the
thrust requirements of the plane, which reduces

00:06:08.040 --> 00:06:12.840
it’s fuel consumption, reducing the sweep
reduces the wing area, which again reduces

00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:13.840
the drag.

00:06:13.840 --> 00:06:18.910
So reducing the speed by just 10% results
in a much larger percentage of in fuel savings.

00:06:18.910 --> 00:06:22.010
Consider that if you were flying on a 3 hour
flight this would increase your flight time

00:06:22.010 --> 00:06:26.360
by just 18 minutes and this increased transit
time would be even less of an issue when you

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:31.260
factor in the reduced boarding times that
the double aisle configuration facilitates.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:35.130
The next design iteration moved engines from
under the wing to the rear of the plane and

00:06:35.130 --> 00:06:39.160
mounted the engines flush with the fuselage,
but this requires some future tech that isn’t

00:06:39.160 --> 00:06:40.160
quite ready.

00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:43.840
With the current configuration, engines are
placed far from the body of the plane and

00:06:43.840 --> 00:06:46.640
so the air entering them is undisturbed and
uniform.

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:50.440
This is ideal for the engine designers because
each of the blades in the compressor experience

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:53.190
the same air pressure and speed through each
cycle.

00:06:53.190 --> 00:06:56.760
But if we move the engines tight against the
back of the plane the engines have to ingest

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:00.370
the boundary layer air-flow, which is the
slow moving layer of air that builds up on

00:07:00.370 --> 00:07:02.020
the surface of the plane.

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:06.030
This type of engine is called a boundary layer
ingestion engine and it has been a topic of

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:10.260
great interest for NASA and other aerospace
companies, because it reduces the loss of

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:12.580
kinetic energy of the aircraft greatly.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:16.550
In a normal plane this boundary layer of slow
moving air simply rolls of the back of the

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:19.130
plane and mixes with the fast moving air.

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:23.380
This causes vortices and a low pressure zone
behind the plane, which creates drag.

00:07:23.380 --> 00:07:28.770
The idea behind the BLI engines is that they
take this slow moving air and speed it up

00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:30.340
and thus eliminate some of that drag.

00:07:30.340 --> 00:07:33.160
It’s a nice idea that is far from being
ready.

00:07:33.160 --> 00:07:36.840
The first problem we face is that non-uniform
air entering the engines.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:40.610
The air entering the engine furthest from
the fuselage of the plane is moving faster

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:43.090
than the air entering the engine near the
surface.

00:07:43.090 --> 00:07:47.560
This creates a discontinuity of stress, as
discussed before in my dreamliner window video,

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:52.450
cycling high and low stresses is VERY bad
for any part, as it results in fatigue of

00:07:52.450 --> 00:07:56.760
the part and when your part is rotating through
those high and low stresses a few thousand

00:07:56.760 --> 00:08:00.470
times per minute...your part isn’t going
to last very long and that’s just problem

00:08:00.470 --> 00:08:01.470
number one.

00:08:01.470 --> 00:08:03.590
The next big problem is stall.

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:07.210
Airflow normally moves uniformly through a
jet engine, but when it’s distorted as it

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:10.480
enters the engine, there’s a high risk of
compressor stall.

00:08:10.480 --> 00:08:14.240
Compressor stall works similarly stall on
a wing, where the speed and angle of attack

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:17.130
of the wing can result in flow separation
behind the wing.

00:08:17.130 --> 00:08:20.840
This prevents the wing from generating lift
and thus stall occurs.

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:24.570
Non-uniform, turbulent air makes this far
more likely to occur.

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:28.580
When this happens in a compressor it can lead
to a chain reaction of stall, as the localised

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:33.210
stagnated air travels with the blade it stalled
on, but lags behind slightly allowing it to

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:36.590
come in contact with other blades, which then
stall too.

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:40.849
Compressor stall may just result in localised
areas of stall that affect the engine's performance

00:08:40.849 --> 00:08:45.610
or it can result in a complete flow reversal
where the incoming air is not being compressed

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:50.769
enough to work against the previously compressed
air which results in an explosive flow reversal

00:08:50.769 --> 00:08:53.360
with air coming out the inlet of the engine.

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:57.480
For these embedded engines to ever make their
way onto a commercial aircraft significant

00:08:57.480 --> 00:09:01.699
leaps in airflow prediction and engine design
&amp; control will be needed.

00:09:01.699 --> 00:09:05.899
Although there are technical challenges, their
use could offer significant reduction in fuel

00:09:05.899 --> 00:09:08.920
consumption over the current generation of
podded engines.

00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:13.079
All of these technologies combined in the
D8 have been calculated to have a potential

00:09:13.079 --> 00:09:18.130
fuel savings of nearly 50% over conventional
technology and with the continual rise of

00:09:18.130 --> 00:09:19.370
fuel prices.

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:40.639
This plane could be making it’s way to an
airport near 

00:09:40.639 --> 00:10:28.899
you sooner than you may think.

